      "kind": "default",
      "eyebrow": "LOLER 1998",
      "heading": "Thorough examination of lifting equipment",
      "html": "<p>The Lifting Operations and Lifting Equipment Regulations 1998 need lifting equipment to get a thorough examination by a competent person. Regulation 9 sets at least every six months for equipment used to lift people and for lifting accessories, and at least every twelve months for other lifting equipment, or in accordance with an examination scheme. It covers cranes, hoists, lifts, slings and chains.</p><p>Quickler records each item, its result and any defect, with how serious it is and a photo. Then it writes the report. It does not make equipment safe. Check the current LOLER requirements.</p>"
    },
    {
      "kind": "default",
      "eyebrow": "PSSR 2000",
      "heading": "Pressure systems and the written scheme",
      "html": "<p>Regulation 8 of the Pressure Systems Safety Regulations 2000 says the user of an installed system, or the owner of a mobile one, must not operate it without a written scheme for periodic examination by a competent person. The scheme covers protective devices and any vessel or pipework where a defect could cause danger. A competent person draws it up or certifies it as suitable, and reviews it at appropriate intervals. Regulation 9 says the parts in the scheme must be examined by a competent person in accordance with it, with a written report within 28 days. You look for corrosion, cracking and wastage.</p><p>Quickler ties the examination to the scheme and its due dates, records your findings with photos, and writes the report. It does not draw up the scheme or say the system is safe to run. Check the current PSSR requirements.</p>"
    },
    {
      "kind": "default",
      "eyebrow": "Defects and duties",
      "heading": "Reporting a danger to people",
      "html": "<p>Under LOLER regulation 10, the person making a thorough examination must notify the employer forthwith of any defect that is or could become a danger to people. Where there is an existing or imminent risk of serious personal injury, they must also send a copy of the report to the enforcing authority as soon as is practicable. The software does not decide that. You do.</p><p>Quickler helps by recording the finding, how serious it is and the timescale on the spot, with a photo. The decision to report, and to whom, stays with you.</p>"
