      "html": "<p>LOLER 1998 regulation 9 sets thorough examination intervals: at least every six months for equipment used to lift people and for lifting accessories, at least every twelve months for other lifting equipment, or in accordance with an examination scheme. Regulation 10 requires the person making the examination to notify the employer forthwith of a defect that is or could become a danger to persons, and to send a copy of the report to the enforcing authority as soon as practicable where there is an existing or imminent risk of serious personal injury. Regulation 11 sets how long reports are kept. PSSR 2000 regulation 8 requires a written scheme drawn up or certified by a competent person, and regulation 9 requires examination in accordance with it and a written report within 28 days. The PSSR rules on which systems need a scheme (the definition of relevant fluid and pressure thresholds) and on reporting imminent danger were not read in full, so check the Regulations and HSE guidance. These Regulations apply in Great Britain. Official sources checked on 18 November 2026.</p><ul><li><a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/1998/2307/regulation/9\" rel=\"noopener\">LOLER 1998, regulation 9: thorough examination and inspection (legislation.gov.uk)</a></li><li><a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/1998/2307/regulation/10\" rel=\"noopener\">LOLER 1998, regulation 10: reports and defects (legislation.gov.uk)</a></li><li><a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/1998/2307/regulation/11\" rel=\"noopener\">LOLER 1998, regulation 11: keeping of information (legislation.gov.uk)</a></li><li><a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/2000/128/regulation/8\" rel=\"noopener\">PSSR 2000, regulation 8: written scheme of examination (legislation.gov.uk)</a></li><li><a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/2000/128/regulation/9\" rel=\"noopener\">PSSR 2000, regulation 9: examination in accordance with the scheme (legislation.gov.uk)</a></li></ul>